# Copy to .env. Docker Compose reads it; git and Docker both ignore it.
#
# Names only in this file, never values. The repository is public.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- providers
#
# Optional. PSOK reads these through providers.yaml's api_key_env, so a key
# given this way needs neither the OS keychain nor the secrets file. You can
# also add keys from the interface once it is running, which is the usual way.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
GROQ_API_KEY=

# ------------------------------------------------------- app registrations
#
# The OAuth client PSOK identifies *itself* with. This is the app's identity,
# never an account's: whoever runs this container still signs in with their own
# Google or Spotify account, and their tokens stay in their own volume. Nobody
# reaches anybody else's mail by sharing a client id.
#
# Left blank on purpose, and blank is a working configuration -- connectors
# that need one will say so and link their setup page.
#
# Before filling these in, know what a shared client actually buys, because it
# is less than it sounds:
#
#   Google  -- an app in Testing requires you to add every user by email in the
#              Cloud console (100 max), and Google expires a test user's grant
#              after 7 days, refresh token included. Publishing is blocked
#              twice over: Gmail scopes are restricted and need a paid CASA
#              assessment, and Branding needs a domain you can verify in Search
#              Console. So a shared client removes setup for you and changes
#              nothing for anyone else.
#   Spotify -- the same shape, milder: 25 users in Development Mode, each added
#              by email in the dashboard.
#
# There is deliberately no GitHub slot. A GitHub OAuth App needs its client
# secret at token exchange, so a default would mean handing that secret to
# everyone. It cannot read anyone's repositories, but it authorises the app --
# and a leaked one lets a stranger run a convincing consent screen under your
# app's name. A GitHub App with device flow is the right fix, and is not built.
#
# Connectors that need none of this and work out of the box: fetch, memory,
# playwright, chrome-devtools, Vercel (it supports dynamic registration), and
# Microsoft To Do (Microsoft's own public client, device code -- you sign into
# your own Microsoft account and PSOK stores no client credential at all).
PSOK_DEFAULT_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
PSOK_DEFAULT_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
PSOK_DEFAULT_SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=
PSOK_DEFAULT_SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=
